<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Visiting Our Country – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f8f8f8;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f8f8f8;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Interesting Things to See</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f8f8f8;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f8f8f8;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Distinguish between facts and opinions in texts on everyday subjects. Reading: Analyze the similarities and differences between two texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in a travel guide and explain how these impact visitor perceptions (e.g., "The guide says Panama hats are popular souvenirs, but this might be an opinion to promote local businesses."). Can compare two guides on Panama City attractions (e.g., "Both guides say you have to try the restaurants. The local food is amazing."). Can analyze opinion differences between two restaurant reviews (e.g., "One reviewer loved the food, but didn't like the decorations. The other reviewer loved everything about the restaurant.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a map of Panama City with images of famous landmarks like the Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, and Amador Causeway. Students are asked to identify the locations. The teacher asks, "Which of these places have you visited?" and "Which do you recommend a tourist to see?" using target vocabulary like 'visit', 'recommend', 'attraction', 'must-see'. This activates prior knowledge and sets the context of 'Visiting Our Country'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe an attraction and give a recommendation, distinguishing between a fact and an opinion. For example, "The Panama Canal connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans (fact). It's a breathtaking engineering marvel (opinion)." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/) and contrastive sounds /st/ in 'stay' and /ks/ in 'explain'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'Casco Viejo was built in 1673' a fact or an opinion?" (Expected answer: A fact.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'The food at that restaurant is wonderful,' am I stating a fact or an opinion?" (Expected answer: An opinion.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short excerpts (200-250 words each) from online travel guides or blog posts describing two different attractions or restaurants in Panama City. For example, one guide describing Casco Viejo and another reviewing a local seafood market restaurant. The texts will incorporate vocabulary like 'area', 'attraction', 'history', 'market', 'restaurant', 'explore', 'showcase', 'breathtaking', 'unique', 'must-see', 'local', 'famous'. Grammar will include past simple/present perfect (e.g., "Casco Viejo was once neglected, but it has become popular") and some reported speech if reviewers quoted others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Can distinguish between factual information and opinions in texts on everyday subjects and compare information from different sources, specifically travel guides or reviews.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of each text?" (e.g., Text 1: To describe Casco Viejo, Text 2: To review a restaurant). "What kind of visitor would be interested in each place?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read statements about the texts and mark them as True or False, correcting the false ones. (e.g., "Casco Viejo is a modern area. True/False?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match specific details (e.g., location, type of food, historical fact) to the correct text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Reading.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying facts vs. opinions, comparing information. Grammar: Past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions over time (e.g., "Casco Viejo was once a neglected area, but after its restoration, it has become a popular destination for tourists."); Reported speech (e.g., "They said that the best seafood is found in the seafood market."). Vocabulary: 'area', 'location', 'attraction', 'history', 'market', 'museum', 'restaurant', 'landmark', 'guide', 'explore', 'showcase', 'witness', 'recommend', 'describe', 'enjoy', 'breathtaking', 'unique', 'wonderful', 'must-see', 'local', 'famous'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – Fact or Opinion?:</b> The teacher provides a list of sentences extracted directly from the two reading texts. Students work individually to label each sentence as a 'Fact' or 'Opinion'. For example, "The Panama Canal generates significant revenue for the country" (Fact) vs. "Eating fresh seafood at the market is a wonderful experience" (Opinion).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – Grammar and Vocabulary Application:</b> Students complete sentences based on the texts, using target grammar and vocabulary. For instance, "Casco Viejo ________ a neglected area in the past, but it ________ a popular attraction for tourists since its restoration (was, has become)." or "The reviewer ________ the seafood ________ amazing (said, was)." Students might also complete a Venn diagram comparing the two texts, filling in shared facts/opinions and unique ones.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction – Compare and Discuss:</b> In pairs, students discuss their answers for the fact/opinion activity and the Venn diagram. They use phrases like "I think this is a fact because..." or "I believe this is an opinion because..." They also discuss similarities and differences between the two texts, using comparison language. (e.g., "Both guides say you have to try the restaurants. The local food is amazing.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group discussion leading to a comparative recommendation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of 3-4. They imagine they are working for a local tourism agency and have received inquiries from two different types of tourists: one interested in history and culture, and another interested in local food and vibrant atmospheres. Using the two travel guide/review texts from Stage 2, each group must compare the texts, identify facts and opinions, and then prepare a short oral recommendation for each type of tourist. They should explain *why* they recommend one place over the other based on their textual analysis. They must integrate grammar (past simple/present perfect, future with will/going to) and vocabulary (e.g., 'plan', 'itinerary', 'recommend', 'enjoy', 'explore', 'unique', 'tranquil', 'vibrant', 'must-see'). They should also practice asking for and giving recommendations (Pragmatic skill: "What places should my friend visit?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students directly apply their reading comprehension skills to the scenario of 'Visiting Our Country' and the theme 'Interesting Things to See' by critically analyzing and comparing information from tourist resources to make informed recommendations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment during group discussions and recommendations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their comparative recommendation to the class. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual and group performance. Peers can use a simplified checklist to evaluate other groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., 'interesting', 'restaurant', 'showcase').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary from the reading texts (e.g., 'attraction', 'landmark', 'recommend', 'breathtaking', 'unique').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in distinguishing between facts and opinions in their recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective comparison of the two texts, highlighting similarities and differences to support recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group, offering directions and suggestions clearly (Sociolinguistic skill).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1 to 5, how confident do you feel now distinguishing facts from opinions in a travel guide?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about comparing texts today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did identifying facts and opinions help you understand the travel guides better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of comparing the two texts did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The ability of students to differentiate facts from opinions was good, but some struggled with complex reported speech structures. The group discussions were lively, with most students actively participating and using target vocabulary. However, a few groups found it difficult to clearly articulate the 'why' behind their recommendations without simply restating text details. More scaffolding for sentence starters for comparative analysis and recommendation might be needed next time. Pronunciation of 'interesting' and 'restaurant' improved after modeling, but 'showcase' still needs attention.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading comprehension and critical analysis of texts. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the factual and opinion-based information they've learned and compared about Panamanian attractions to actively engage in discussions and role-plays, practicing asking for and giving recommendations and offering directions in a communicative setting. They will verbally "showcase" their knowledge from the reading tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>